WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:08.060
Opioid Buster 1: Stop right there!

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.009
Okay, good.

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:11.009
No signs of overdose.

00:00:11.009 --> 00:00:12.830
Opioid Buster 2: No need for Naloxone!

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:14.520
Girl Addict: Nalo- What?

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.380
Opioid Buster 1: Naloxone.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:17.620
It treats opioid overdoses.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.750
Opioid Buster 2: Yeah, look on the screen.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:23.190
It displaces the opioids that bind to the
opioid receptors in the central nervous system.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:24.910
Weren’t you done with using Morphine?

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:26.280
Girl: Wait a minute!

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.500
Who are you guys?!

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:28.720
Opioid Buster 2: Don’t worry about us.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:29.779
Put that down!

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:31.539
You’ve become dependent on Opioids.

00:00:31.539 --> 00:00:33.170
Girl: What can I say?

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.880
A little extra medication never hurt anyone?

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.680
Opioid Buster 1: Wrong.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.309
In 2017, 47,000 Americans died from opioid
overdoses and 1.7 million were abusing prescribed

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:44.470
opioid painkillers.

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:45.470
Look here!

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:49.780
Opioid Buster 1: See, there are two types
of agonists for opioid receptors.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.920
Opioid Buster 2: We have the endogenous type
,which are naturally made within your body

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.879
and here we have exogenous opioids, which
come from nature and are man-made.

00:00:55.879 --> 00:01:02.570
OP1: Both types bind to the receptors, like
the kappa-opioid receptor, to relieve pain.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.320
Girl: Wait, how do they do this?

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.320
OP2: GPCR.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:06.320
Opioid receptors are coupled to G Proteins.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.700
When the G protein is activated, it will undergo
a shape change, causing certain activities

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.280
in the cell to produce an effect.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.710
OP1: Opioids achieve their effects by preventing
neurotransmitter release.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:19.640
After the opioid binds to the receptor, part
of the G Protein will bind with the voltage

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.640
gated calcium channel.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.250
Calcium is important for signaling, AKA communication
throughout your body.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.280
Without calcium entering the neuron, pain
signaling cannot reach its action potential

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:30.460
and pain relief occurs.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:34.439
Girl: Wow, so that’s how these pills work.

00:01:34.439 --> 00:01:36.650
What happens if I continue taking opioids?

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:40.869
OP2: It will continue engaging with the kappa
opioid receptors and can lead to depression

00:01:40.869 --> 00:01:43.110
since dopamine levels decrease in your body.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:46.580
Opioid Buster 1: Don’t worry, talking to
your doctor can help you face your addiction.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:49.850
Opioid Buster 2: There are also medication
assisted treatments (MATs) that involve the

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.759
usage of both counseling and medicine therapies.

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:53.840
Opioid Buster 1: You can get through this!

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.220
Now drop the medication!

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.509
Girl: Huh?!?

00:01:56.509 --> 00:01:58.439
Where’d they go?

